  "ELEMENT_HINTS": {
    "H": "The lightest element and a nonmetal, it is the most abundant element in the universe",
    "He": "A noble gas and the second-lightest element, it was first detected in the Sun before being found on Earth",
    "Li": "An alkali metal and the lightest solid element, it is widely used in rechargeable batteries",
    "Ne": "A noble gas, it produces the familiar bright red-orange glow used in traditional advertising signs",
    "Na": "An alkali metal, it reacts violently with water and gives table salt its sodium component",
    "Mg": "An alkaline earth metal, it burns with an intense white light and is used in some fireworks and flares",
    "Al": "A post-transition metal and the most abundant metal in Earth's crust, it is valued for being lightweight and corrosion-resistant",
    "Si": "A metalloid and semiconductor, it is the key material used in most modern computer chips",
    "Cl": "A halogen and highly reactive nonmetal, it is widely used to disinfect drinking water",
    "Ar": "A noble gas and chemically inert element, it is commonly used to provide an unreactive atmosphere for welding",
    "K": "An alkali metal, it is essential to living cells and gives bananas and other foods their potassium content",
    "Fe": "A transition metal and the main component of steel, it is also the element that makes blood's hemoglobin able to carry oxygen",
    "Cu": "A transition metal, it is one of the best electrical conductors and has given its name to the color copper",
    "Br": "A halogen and the only nonmetal that is liquid at room temperature, it is a dark reddish-brown liquid",
    "Kr": "A noble gas, its name comes from a Greek word meaning 'hidden'",
    "Ag": "A transition metal and the best electrical conductor of any element, it is the shiny metal used in many pieces of jewelry",
    "W": "A transition metal with the highest melting point of any element, its symbol comes from the name wolfram",
    "Au": "A transition metal and noble metal, it is famously resistant to corrosion and has the chemical symbol Au from its Latin name",
    "Hg": "A transition metal and the only metallic element that is liquid at room temperature, it was historically used in thermometers",
    "U": "An actinide and naturally radioactive metal, it is the element most associated with the fuel used in nuclear fission reactors"
  },
